.TH xrdfs 1 "@XRootD_VERSION_STRING@"
.SH NAME
xrdfs - xrootd file and directory meta-data utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf

\fBxrdfs\fR \fI[global-options]\fR \fIhost[:port]\fR \fI[command [args]]\fR

\fBxrdfs\fR \fI[global-options]\fR \fIcommand\fR \fI[args containing complete URLs]\fR

\fBcommand\fR: help, cache, chmod, ls, locate, mkdir, mv, stat, statvfs, query,
           rm, rmdir, truncate, prepare, cat, tail, spaceinfo, sum, token, xattr
.fi
.br
.ad l
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBxrdfs\fR utility executes meta-data oriented operations
(e.g., ls, mv, rm, etc.) on one or more xrootd servers.
In batch mode, the endpoint may either be provided separately before the
command or inferred from complete URL path operands following the command.
Global options must precede the endpoint or command.
The server-first and command-first forms are alternatives and may not be mixed.
All complete URLs in one command must use the same scheme, user information,
host, and effective port; an omitted default port compares equal to the same
port specified explicitly.
The \fBquery\fR command remains server-first because its implementation-dependent
parameters may themselves look like URLs.
Local \fBfile://\fR and \fBstdio://\fR operands are not remote endpoints and are
rejected in command-first form.
The command-first complete-URL form is supported by cache, cat, chmod, locate,
ls, mkdir, mv, prepare, rm, rmdir, spaceinfo, stat, statvfs, sum, tail, truncate,
token, and xattr.
Command help is available by invoking \fBxrdfs\fR with no command
line options or parameters and then typing "help" in response to the
input prompt.

.SH OPTIONS
\fB-4, --ipv4\fR
.RS 3
Use only the IPv4 network stack and addresses.
.RE
.PP
\fB-6, --ipv6\fR
.RS 3
Use only the IPv6 network stack and addresses.
.RE
.PP
\fB-d, --debug\fR \fIlevel\fR
.RS 3
Set the debug level: 0 off, 1 low, 2 medium, or 3 high.
.RE
.PP
\fB-h, --help\fR
.RS 3
Display help and exit.
.RE
.PP
\fB--no-cwd\fR
.RS 3
Do not preset a CWD in interactive mode.
.RE

.SH EXAMPLES
The following server-first and command-first invocations are equivalent:
.PP
.nf
\fBServer-first\fR                    \fBCommand-first\fR
xrdfs host stat /path           xrdfs stat root://host//path
xrdfs host ls /dir              xrdfs ls root://host//dir
xrdfs host mv /old /new         xrdfs mv root://host//old root://host//new
.fi
.PP
In an XRootD URL, the double slash after the host consists of the slash
separating the authority from the path and the leading slash of the absolute
path. A single slash is also accepted and normalized to an absolute command
path.
.PP
.nf
\fBxrdfs token --validity 30 https://storage.example.org/store/file DOWNLOAD\fR
.fi

.SH COMMANDS
\fBchmod\fR \fIpath\fR \fImode\fR
.br
\fBchmod\fR \fIoctal-mode\fR \fIpath\fR
.RS 3
Modify permissions of the \fIpath\fR. The path-first form accepts either a
nine-character symbolic mode, such as \fIrwxr-x--x\fR, or an unsigned octal
mode no greater than \fI0777\fR. The gfal-compatible mode-first form accepts an
octal mode. The historical path-first form remains supported.

.RE
\fBls\fR \fI[-l]\fR \fI[-u]\fR \fI[-R]\fR \fI[-D]\fR \fI[-Z]\fR \fI[-C]\fR \fI[-h|-H]\fR \fI[-d]\fR \fI[-a]\fR \fI[-1]\fR \fI[--color=never]\fR \fI[--xattr name]\fR \fI[--json]\fR \fI[dirname]\fR
.RS 3
Get directory listing.
.br
\fI-l\fR, \fI--long\fR stat every entry and print long listing
.br
\fI-u\fR print paths as URLs
.br
\fI-R\fR list subdirectories recursively
.br
\fI-D\fR show duplicate entries
.br
\fI-Z\fR check if file is a ZIP archive and if yes list its content
.br
\fI-C\fR checksum every entry
.br
\fI-h\fR, \fI-H\fR, \fI--human-readable\fR print human-readable sizes
.br
\fI-d\fR, \fI--directory\fR list the directory entry instead of its contents
.br
\fI-a\fR, \fI--all\fR accepted for gfal-ls compatibility; xrdfs already includes entries whose names begin with a dot
.br
\fI-1\fR accepted for gfal-ls compatibility; xrdfs already emits one entry per line when long output is not selected
.br
\fI--color=never\fR accepted for gfal-ls compatibility; xrdfs output is already uncolored
.br
\fI--xattr name\fR append the named GFAL virtual or native XRootD attribute to long or JSON output; may be repeated and preserves request order; accepted without visible effect when neither long nor JSON output is selected
.br
\fI--json\fR print one JSON object per directory entry. This implies stat
metadata, suppresses the human-readable listing, and includes values requested
with \fI--xattr\fR in request order. With \fI-d\fR, print one object for the
operand itself. With \fI-u\fR, the \fIpath\fR field contains a complete URL.
For file operands, \fI-C\fR requests and includes the checksum.
.br
\fI--\fR stop option parsing, allowing a path beginning with a dash

.RE
\fBlocate\fR \fI[-n]\fR \fI[-r]\fR \fI[-d]\fR \fI[-m]\fR \fI[-i]\fR \fI[-p]\fR \fI<path>\fR
.RS 3
Get the locations of the path.
.br
\fI-r\fR refresh, don't use cached locations
.br
\fI-n\fR make the server return the response immediately even though it may be incomplete
.br
\fI-d\fR do a recursive, deep locate in order to find data servers
.br
\fI-m\fR prefer host names to IP addresses
.br
\fI-i\fR ignore network dependencies (IPv6/IPv4)
.br
\fI-p\fR be passive: ignore tried/triedrc cgi opaque info


.RE
\fBmkdir\fR \fI[-p|--parents] [-m mode|--mode mode] <dirname>...\fR
.RS 3
Creates one or more directories or directory trees. Every operand is validated
before the first directory is created, and directories are then created in
command-line order.
.br
\fI-p\fR, \fI--parents\fR create the entire directory tree recursively
.br
\fI-m\fR \fImode\fR, \fI--mode\fR \fImode\fR set permissions for newly created
directories. Attached forms such as \fI-m0755\fR and \fI--mode=0755\fR are also
accepted. A mode may be a nine-character symbolic value or an unsigned octal
value no greater than \fI0777\fR. The default remains \fI0750\fR. The native
XRootD server always grants owner read, write, and execute permissions when it
creates a directory; other backends may enforce additional mode policy.
.br
\fI--\fR stop option parsing, allowing a directory name beginning with a dash

.RE
\fBmv\fR \fI<path1> <path2>\fR
.RS 3
Move path1 to path2 locally on the same server.

.RE
\fBstat\fR \fI[--json]\fR \fI[-q flags]\fR \fI[--]\fR \fI<path>\fR \fI[<paths>]\fR
.RS 3
Get info about the file(s) or directory(ies).
.br
\fI--json\fR print one JSON object per path without the leading blank lines or
human-readable field labels. Query checks selected with \fI-q\fR still affect
the exit status.
.br
\fI-q\fR \fB<flags>\fR Makes
xrdfs return error code 55 to the shell if the
requested flag combination is not present;
flags may be combined together using '|' or '&'
.br
Available flags:
\fBXBitSet\fR, \fBIsDir\fR, \fBOther\fR, \fBOffline\fR, \fBPOSCPending\fR,
\fBIsReadable\fR, \fBIsWritable\fR, \fBBackUpExists\fR
.br
\fI--\fR stop option parsing, allowing a path beginning with a dash
.br
Each JSON object contains \fIpath\fR, \fItype\fR, \fIsize\fR, integer
\fImtime\fR, \fIatime\fR, and \fIctime\fR values, numeric \fIflags\fR,
\fIflag_names\fR, \fIextended\fR, nullable \fImode\fR, \fIpermissions\fR,
\fIowner\fR, \fIgroup\fR, and \fIchecksum\fR fields, and an ordered
\fIxattrs\fR array. Metadata unavailable without an extended stat response is
represented by null fields; unavailable access and change times are zero.
\fImode\fR is the raw octal mode string and \fIpermissions\fR is its symbolic
nine-character representation. JSON strings must be valid UTF-8; invalid input
causes the command to fail rather than silently replacing bytes.

.RE
\fBstatvfs\fR \fI<path>\fR
.RS 3
Get info about a virtual file system.

.RE
\fBquery\fR \fI<code> <params>\fR
.RS 3
Obtain server information. Query codes:
.br
\fIconfig\fR         \fB<what>\fR   Server configuration; <what> is one of the following:
.RS 5
bind_max      - the maximum number of parallel streams
.br
chksum        - the supported checksum
.br
pio_max       - maximum number of parallel I/O requests
.br
readv_ior_max - maximum size of a readv element
.br
readv_iov_max - maximum number of readv entries
.br
tpc           - support for third party copies
.br
wan_port      - the port to use for wan copies
.br
wan_window    - the wan_port window size
.br
window        - the tcp window size
.br
cms           - the status of the cmsd
.br
role          - the role in a cluster
.br
sitename      - the site name
.br
version       - the version of the server
.br
.RE
\fIchecksumcancel\fR \fB<path>\fR   File checksum cancellation
.br
\fIchecksum\fR       \fB<path>\fR   File checksum
.br
\fIopaque\fR         \fB<arg>\fR    Implementation dependent
.br
\fIopaquefile\fR     \fB<arg>\fR    Implementation dependent
.br
\fIspace\fR          \fB<space>\fR  Logical space stats
.br
\fIstats\fR          \fB<what>\fR   Server stats; <what> is a list of letters
indicating information to be returned:
.RS 5
a - all statistics
.br
p - protocol statistics
.br
b - buffer usage statistics
.br
s - scheduling statistics
.br
d - device polling statistics
.br
u - usage statistics
.br
i - server identification
.br
z - synchronized statistics
.br
l - connection statistics
.br
.RE
\fIxattr\fR          \fB<path>\fR   Extended attributes
.br
\fIprepare\fR        \fB[-d|--delete] <reqid> [filenames]\fR  Prepare request status or deletion
.br
\fItape\fR           \fB[discover | archiveinfo <paths...> | delete <reqid>]\fR  WLCG Tape REST queries
.br
\fIarchiveinfo\fR    \fB<paths...>\fR  Archive locality and tape metadata query


.RE
\fBrm\fR \fI[-r|-R|--recursive]\fR \fI[-f|--force]\fR \fI[--dry-run]\fR \fI[--]\fR
\fI<path>\fR \fI[<paths>]\fR
.RS 3
Remove one or more files. For HTTP(S) and DAV(S) endpoints, each target is
verified not to be a collection before any DELETE request is sent. This avoids
using WebDAV's recursive collection DELETE for a non-recursive file removal.
.br
\fI-r\fR, \fI-R\fR, \fI--recursive\fR remove directories and their contents.
Native XRootD trees are traversed iteratively in postorder with existing
\fIRm\fR, \fIDirList\fR, and \fIRmDir\fR operations. File removal is attempted
first, and directory symlinks are never followed. A successful WebDAV
collection DELETE completes the recursive operand without client traversal.
Partial or ambiguous responses fail closed.
.br
\fI-f\fR, \fI--force\fR ignore nonexistent files and arguments, matching POSIX and gfal-rm
.br
Every recursive operand is validated before the first mutation. Empty paths,
the namespace root, and paths containing dot or dot-dot traversal components
are rejected. URL query parameters are preserved while addressing descendants.
.br
If one top-level tree fails, its remaining children are not attempted, but
later top-level operands are still processed. The first failure is returned;
multi-path recursive removal is therefore not transactional.
.br
\fI--dry-run\fR inspect every target without changing remote storage. Files
that would be removed are printed with \fISKIP\fR; recursively planned
directories are printed with \fISKIP DIR\fR after their children. Missing
targets are printed with \fIMISSING\fR. Dry-run performs only metadata
operations (\fIStat\fR and, for recursive directories, \fIDirList\fR); it
never calls \fIRm\fR or \fIRmDir\fR and never sends WebDAV DELETE. A failed
tree is abandoned, later top-level operands are still planned, and the first
failure is returned. Query parameters are retained for descendant metadata
requests, while \fIauthz\fR values are redacted from output and diagnostics.
.br
XrdCl does not provide an lstat operation. Metadata inspection can therefore
follow a directory symlink, so a dry-run directory-symlink plan is advisory.
To keep metadata planning finite even for cyclic or hostile listings, dry-run
fails a tree that would descend beyond 4096 directory levels. The actual
recursive removal path retains its non-following symlink checks and is not
subject to this dry-run-only limit.
.br
\fI--\fR stop option parsing, allowing a path beginning with a dash.

.RE
\fBrmdir\fR \fI<dirname>\fR
.RS 3
Remove an empty directory. For HTTP(S) and DAV(S) endpoints, the target is
verified to be an empty collection before DELETE is sent.

.RE
\fBtruncate\fR \fI<filename> <length>\fR
.RS 3
Truncate a file.

.RE
\fBprepare\fR \fI[-c|--colocate]\fR \fI[-f|--fresh]\fR \fI[-s|--stage]\fR \fI[-w|--write]\fR \fI[-e|--evict|--release]\fR \fI[-p|--priority priority]\fR \fI[-a|--abort|--cancel requestid]\fR \fI[--pin-lifetime duration]\fR \fI[--metadata json]\fR \fI[--wait]\fR \fI[--timeout seconds]\fR \fI[--]\fR \fIfilenames\fR
.RS 3
Prepare one or more files for access or stage them from tape.
.br
\fI-c\fR, \fI--colocate\fR co-locate staged files if possible
.br
\fI-f\fR, \fI--fresh\fR refresh file access time even if the location is known
.br
\fI-s\fR, \fI--stage\fR stage the files to disk if they are not online
.br
\fI-w\fR, \fI--write\fR when files will be accessed for modification
.br
\fI-p\fR, \fI--priority\fR priority of the request, 0 (lowest) - 3 (highest)
.br
\fI-a\fR, \fI--abort\fR, \fI--cancel\fR abort/cancel stage request
.br
\fI-e\fR, \fI--evict\fR, \fI--release\fR evict/release the file from disk cache
.br
\fI--pin-lifetime duration\fR desired disk pin duration (seconds or ISO-8601 duration like PT24H)
.br
\fI--metadata json\fR targeted storage metadata for stage request
.br
\fI--wait\fR poll synchronously until staging is completed or failed
.br
\fI--timeout seconds\fR maximum polling timeout in seconds
.br
\fI--\fR stop option parsing, allowing a path beginning with a dash

.RE
\fBcat\fR \fI[-b|--bytes]\fR \fI[-o localfile]\fR \fI[--]\fR \fIfiles\fR
.RS 3
Print contents of one or more files to stdout
.br
\fI-b\fR, \fI--bytes\fR accepted for gfal-cat compatibility; output is always byte-preserving
.br
\fI-o\fR print to the specified local file
.br
\fI--\fR stop option parsing, allowing a path beginning with a dash

.RE
\fBtail\fR \fI[-c bytes] [-f]\fR \fIfile\fR
.RS 3
Output last part of files to stdout.
.br
\fI-c\fR num_bytes out last num_bytes
.br
\fI-f\fR           output appended data as file grows

.RE
\fBtoken\fR \fI[-w|--write]\fR \fI[--validity minutes]\fR \fI[--issuer URL]\fR \fI[--]\fR \fIpath\fR \fI[activity ...]\fR
.RS 3
Request a token for a storage path and print it to standard output.
The storage endpoint must use HTTPS or DAVS. Without explicit activities, the
request uses LIST and DOWNLOAD for read access; write access also adds MANAGE,
UPLOAD, and DELETE.
.br
\fI-w\fR, \fI--write\fR request write access instead of read access
.br
\fI--validity minutes\fR request a nonnegative validity in minutes (default: 60)
.br
\fI--issuer URL\fR request the token through an explicit HTTPS or DAVS issuer. The
client discovers an OAuth token endpoint using RFC 8414 and OpenID metadata,
then tries the supported SciTokens and macaroon flows. If issuer discovery is
unavailable, the request falls back to direct storage issuance. Omit this
option to request a storage-issued token directly
.br
\fI--\fR stop option parsing, allowing a path beginning with a dash

.RE
\fBspaceinfo\fR \fIpath\fR
.RS 3
Get space statistics for given path.

.RE
\fBsum\fR \fIpath checksum_type\fR
.RS 3
Query the file checksum using the requested algorithm. The positional layout
matches \fBgfal-sum\fR; for example, \fBxrdfs sum URL ADLER32\fR.

.RE
\fBxattr\fR \fI<path> [virtual-attribute]\fR
.br
\fBxattr\fR \fI<path> -- <virtual-attribute>\fR
.br
\fBxattr\fR \fI<path> <code> <params>\fR
.RS 3
With no attribute, query the fixed GFAL virtual XRootD attributes
\fIxroot.cksum\fR, \fIxroot.space\fR, \fIxroot.xattr\fR, and
\fIspacetoken\fR. With one virtual attribute, query its value. This shorthand
is a thin adapter over existing checksum, space, and stat operations; it does
not list native XRootD file attributes. In addition to the fixed names,
\fIuser.checksum.<algorithm>\fR selects a checksum algorithm and
\fIuser.status\fR maps the stat Offline and BackUpExists flags to GFAL's
ONLINE, NEARLINE, ONLINE_AND_NEARLINE, or UNKNOWN values. An unrecognized
single attribute name falls back to the native XRootD get operation and prints
the raw value.
.br
The XRootD-specific virtual names are available for XRootD protocols. For
HTTP(S) and WebDAV URLs, the bare virtual list instead reports the discovered
\fItaperestapi.version\fR, \fItaperestapi.uri\fR, and
\fItaperestapi.sitename\fR values. On those protocols, \fIuser.status\fR queries
Tape REST archive locality and maps it to the same GFAL status values. The
checksum virtual attribute can also be used through a different protocol plugin
when that plugin implements checksum queries.
.br
Use \fI--\fR before a virtual attribute name that would otherwise be parsed as
an explicit code.
.br
The explicit codes operate on native XRootD file attributes:
.br
\fIset\fR         \fB<attr>\fR   Set extended attribute; 
                     <attr> is string of form name=value
.br
\fIget\fR         \fB<name>\fR   Get extended attribute.
.br
\fIdel\fR         \fB<name>\fR   Delete extended attribute.
.br
\fIlist\fR                 List extended attributes.

.SH RETURN CODES
.RE
\fB50\fR  : generic error (e.g. config, internal, data, OS)

\fB51\fR  : socket related error

\fB52\fR  : postmaster related error

\fB53\fR  : XRootD related error

\fB54\fR  : redirection error

\fB55\fR  : query response was negative (this is not an error)

.SH NOTES
For the list of available environment variables please refer to xrdcopy(1)

.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors yield an error message and a non-zero exit status.

